<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="three-contexts" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Three Contexts: Structure of the Program, Pedagogical Practices, and Curriculum</title>

  <subsection xml:id="context-structure">
    <title>Structure of the Program</title>

    <p>
      This is the most fundamental context, as it sets the tone for everything else in terms of
      expectations. It encompasses the basics of how the program is run, the logistical elements,
      and the pedagogical goals. It also helps that good practices are <em>sustained</em>, and
      don't depend necessarily on particular individuals.
    </p>

    <p>
      The structure of the program includes elements like where the program is held, how often it
      runs, what kinds of people work with the program, which mathematical resources (such as
      manipulatives) can be provided, how are students organized (for example, do they work in
      small groups?), which nature of mentor-students relations will be pursued, which cognitive
      demand in the activities is expected, and what are the explicit and implicit expectations
      for mentor practices. The program structure is often designed without noticing how it might
      impact the student experience. Changing aspects of how the program is run can positively
      impact other activities and outcomes.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="context-pedagogical">
    <title>Pedagogical Practices</title>

    <p>
      While the curriculum lays the foundation for culturally responsive practice in a program,
      the pedagogical practices used when teaching the activities are what make those practices
      visible to the students. We can embed these practices in the activities, but unless they are
      activated when working with students, they cannot be executed at full capacity and to the
      fullest intended quality. So, mentors are responsible for making CRT alive and thus have
      the largest impact on the students.
    </p>

    <p>
      Of particular importance is that often there are different groups of people designing
      activities, and implementing them. It is key that there is communication on what the goals
      of the activity are and what is important to do with the students. It is also key that
      mentors have the time to anticipate obstacles that could happen during the learning session,
      and how to prepare for them: the curriculum will provide some answers, but not all, and
      mentors will need to rely on their peers and a supporting program structure to succeed.
      (For example, useful assessments to gain conceptual knowledge, opportunities to learn about
      multiple representations, time to practice the session and anticipate misconceptions, time
      to think about their particular students and how they will react to the context, etc.)
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="context-curriculum">
    <title>Curriculum / Activity Design</title>

    <p>
      Core to any program is the design of the activities it runs. This is an essential place to
      encode culturally responsive practices because it sets the tone for everything that happens
      afterward. However, it is important to remember that the design of the curriculum is not
      everything <mdash/> just because an activity includes certain practices it doesn't mean
      they will be done in practice. In other words, a powerful curriculum far from guarantees
      that it will translate to excellence implementing it. An intentional effort must be made for
      this, which includes constant preparation. At that point it comes down to the program
      structure and pedagogical practices.
    </p>

    <p>
      Conversely, starting with a curriculum that includes few opportunities for culturally
      relevant practices will most likely set low bar expectations for the mentors, and it will
      require a big effort for mentors to adjust and incorporate CRT. For this, we believe that
      it's critical for the curriculum to include and show all the CRT goals that are expected,
      rather than expecting mentors to <q>fix</q> it on the fly. If the curriculum shows
      limitations, then it should be modified to address them, for next time it's implemented.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="context-aligning">
    <title>Aligning the Three Contexts</title>

    <p>
      A culturally responsive after-school program will apply all six dimensions within each of
      the three contexts <mdash/> each dimension must be realized to carry a practice all the way
      to student growth. For example, we could design an activity to foster interpersonal
      relationships between students and mentors, but if mentors are not trained to properly run
      the activity it may not perform as expected. Therefore, not only do we need to apply each
      dimension at each level, but also make sure that the efforts at each level are in concert.
      A key good practice in this regard is to document the goals and expectations at each
      context, see how they interact, and assess if they are realistic, or which changes need to
      be made. Reflection sessions should be held on a regular basis, as a way to effectively
      change practices.
    </p>

    <p>
      One frequent error is making changes at each context but in different, disconnected or
      incompatible ways (sometimes even contradictory). You may still see some benefit in this
      case, but those changes are likely not as impactful as they could be, and may not be long
      term ones.
    </p>
  </subsection>

</section>
